flowchart LR

IN2(["`◀ from 2 · API`"])

CONCEPT["`WHAT A VECTOR IS`"]
CONCEPT_N["`'normalizacao de banco de dados'
│
▼  bge-m3
▂▇▃▁▅▇▂▆▁▃▇▄▂▅▇▁▃▆▂▇▄▁▅▃
[ 0.021, -0.184, 0.077, ... ]
1024 float32
───────────
the model never returns words. It returns a
POSITION in a 1024-dimension space, and the
only question anyone asks of it afterwards is
how close two positions are`"]
CONCEPT -.- CONCEPT_N

TXT[/"`Markdown text`"/]
CHK["`Chunk`"]
CHK_N["`embedding_chunk_chars = 1000, header-aware
the breadcrumb rides with the chunk, so a
retrieved passage still knows its section
a 120 KB book ≈ 120000 chars ≈ 120 chunks`"]
CHK -.- CHK_N

BAT["`Batch of 8`"]
BAT_N["`embedding_batch_size = 8 · ~40 s per request
120 chunks ÷ 8 = 15 requests ≈ 10 min
sized so ONE request finishes inside a timeout:
all 120 in one call never returns`"]
BAT -.- BAT_N

CACHE[("`Redis
embedding cache`")]
CACHE_N["`TTL 604800 s = 7 days
identical text is never embedded twice`"]
CACHE -.- CACHE_N

EMB{{"`bge-m3 · 1024-d`"}}
EMB_N["`1.5 s/chunk on CPU vs 0.06 s for all-minilm.
24x slower and kept: the corpus is Portuguese
and the fast ones are English-trained.
cost paid ONCE per document, against retrieval
quality on every plan generated afterwards`"]
EMB -.- EMB_N

VEC[/"`vector(1024)`"/]
VEC_N["`1024 × 4 bytes (float32) = 4 KB per chunk
2000-chunk book = 8 MB of vectors
float32 because pgvector stores float32;
the vectors are the cheap part, time is the cost`"]
VEC -.- VEC_N

IDX[("`chunks
HNSW + TSVECTOR`")]
IDX_N["`ix_chunks_embedding USING hnsw (vector_cosine_ops)
HNSW = a layered proximity graph. Search enters at
a sparse top layer, greedily walks toward the query,
descends. Sub-linear instead of scanning every row.
───────────
it is APPROXIMATE: it can miss a true neighbour.
that is the trade being made, and it is why the
recall numbers below are measured, not assumed.
───────────
content_tsv lives in the same row and commits in
the same transaction: no second store to fall
out of sync at 3am`"]
IDX -.- IDX_N

Q[/"`Question`"/]
QEMB{{"`Embed the question`"}}
QEMB_N["`the SAME model as ingestion. Non-negotiable:
two models = two vector spaces, and a cosine
between them is a meaningless number that still
looks like a score. Nothing errors. It just gets
quietly worse.`"]
QEMB -.- QEMB_N

SEM["`Vector half`"]
SEM_N["`cos(A,B) = (A·B) / (‖A‖ ‖B‖)
distance = 1 − cos
───────────
operator <=> is COSINE DISTANCE: lower is closer.
converted to similarity only at the API edge,
exactly, not rescaled to look nicer.
───────────
cosine and not L2: bge-m3 returns normalised
vectors, so magnitude carries no meaning and
only direction does.
───────────
takes rag_candidate_pool = 30`"]
SEM -.- SEM_N

LEX["`Lexical half`"]
LEX_N["`to_tsvector, lexemes joined by OR, NOT
websearch_to_tsquery, which joins with AND:
'why are food chains short' would demand every
word and match nothing at all.
───────────
OR finds any passage sharing a term; ts_rank_cd
(cover density, proximity-aware) floats the ones
sharing several, close together. That is what
people mean when they say BM25.
───────────
Postgres tokenises with the index configuration,
so stopwords and stemming agree on both sides.
A stopword-only question returns None and the
vector half answers alone: an honest fallback.
Also 30 candidates.`"]
LEX -.- LEX_N

RRF{{"`RRF fusion`"}}
RRF_N["`score = 1/(k + rank_vec) + 1/(k + rank_lex)
rag_rrf_k = 60
───────────
A · vector 1st, lexical 40th → 1/61 + 1/100 = 0.02639
B · vector 5th, lexical 3rd  → 1/65 + 1/63  = 0.03126 ◀ wins
C · vector 2nd, absent       → 1/62 + 0     = 0.01613
───────────
B tops neither list and still wins. Agreement between
two independent searches beats one strong showing,
which is the entire reason to run two.`"]
RRF -.- RRF_N

WHY>"`rank, never score`"]
WHY_N["`cosine distance and ts_rank_cd are different
quantities on different scales with no honest
conversion. Any weighted sum is a magic number
tuned to the corpus it was tuned on.
rank is the one thing both lists genuinely agree
on, which is why this survives a change of
embedding model without retuning.
k = 60 flattens the curve: rank 1 is worth 0.0164
and rank 10 is worth 0.0143, close enough that
agreement outweighs a single first place.`"]
WHY -.- WHY_N

TOPK["`Top 5`"]
TOPK_N["`pool 30 per half against a limit of 5, on purpose:
fusion can only promote what a list returned, so
cutting both at 5 would discard exactly the
agreements this mechanism exists to find`"]
TOPK -.- TOPK_N

CTX[/"`Context block`"/]
CTX_N["`wrapped in untrusted_document_context;
every system prompt treats it as data
closest distance > 0.45 = weak grounding, one of
the two conditions that wake the LLM judge`"]
CTX -.- CTX_N

EVAL["`Measured, not assumed`"]
EVAL_N["`scripts/eval_retrieval.py over a golden set
recall at 1 / 3 / 5, then MRR
───────────
vector  · 0.62 · 0.85 · 0.92 · MRR 0.74
lexical · 0.54 · 0.77 · 0.85 · MRR 0.66
hybrid  · 0.77 · 0.92 · 1.00 · MRR 0.85
───────────
recall and not precision: two irrelevant passages
cost tokens, one missing passage costs a wrong
answer.
expected passages keyed by TEXT, not by chunk id:
ids change on every re-ingestion, and a golden set
you must rebuild is a golden set nobody runs.`"]
EVAL -.- EVAL_N

OUT3(["`▶ continues in 3 · AI`"])
OUT5(["`▶ continues in 5 · DATA`"])

IN2 ==>|"upload"| TXT
TXT --> CHK --> BAT --> CACHE --> EMB --> VEC --> IDX
IDX --> OUT5
CONCEPT -.-> EMB

IN2 ==>|"question"| Q
Q --> QEMB
QEMB ==> SEM
QEMB ==> LEX
IDX -.->|"the rows both halves search"| SEM
IDX -.->|"the rows both halves search"| LEX
SEM ==>|"rank 1..30"| RRF
LEX ==>|"rank 1..30"| RRF
RRF --- WHY
RRF --> TOPK --> CTX
CTX ==> OUT3
EVAL -.->|"is why the read path is hybrid"| RRF

